# OPTIONAL hardware profile for an NVIDIA RTX 4060 laptop (e.g. an MSI gaming
# laptop). This is a SEPARATE, opt-in profile -- it does NOT replace or
# change .env.example in any way, and the Mac/CPU-only defaults in
# llm_decompose.py remain exactly as they are. Nothing here takes effect
# unless you deliberately copy this file to .env (or export these vars
# yourself) on that specific machine.
#
# See SETUP_RTX4060.md for the one-time, machine-specific setup this profile
# depends on (a CUDA-enabled build of llama-cpp-python) -- these env vars
# alone do nothing if that build step hasn't been done; N_GPU_LAYERS would
# silently have no effect on a CPU-only llama-cpp-python build.
#
# To go back to the Mac-safe defaults on ANY machine (including this one if
# the laptop is returned/unavailable): just don't use this file. Deleting it
# or falling back to .env.example fully restores the original behavior --
# nothing outside this file and SETUP_RTX4060.md was touched to add it.

# Bigger than the Mac's default (Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507) -- the RTX 4060's
# 8GB VRAM has room for a meaningfully stronger decomposition model fully
# offloaded, which this project's Mac-class hardware never had. Qwen3-8B
# (unsloth/Qwen3-8B-GGUF) chosen specifically, not just "any bigger model":
# it's the same Qwen3 family/tokenizer/chat-template this codebase already
# targets (the /no_think convention and <think>-tag handling in
# llm_decompose.py's system prompts are literally Qwen3-specific), so no
# prompt-engineering changes are needed to use it -- only the weights
# change. Get the file (verified to exist at this exact name/repo):
#   wget https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3-8B-GGUF/resolve/main/Qwen3-8B-Q4_K_M.gguf
# Q4_K_M (~5GB) leaves comfortable VRAM headroom on an 8GB card once the
# MAX_CONTEXT KV cache and Windows' own GPU/display usage are accounted
# for. Q5_K_M (~5.9GB) is a reasonable step up if `nvidia-smi` shows
# headroom to spare after a Q4_K_M run -- don't jump straight to Q6_K
# (~6.7GB) without checking, it's the one quant level where this card's
# VRAM budget actually gets tight.
#
# MODEL_PATH below must point at wherever you actually saved the file --
# the MODEL_NAME/QUANTIZATION-based default path construction in
# llm_decompose.py expects models/qwen/quantized/<name>-<quant>.gguf,
# which this file's filename doesn't match, so set MODEL_PATH explicitly
# rather than relying on that default.
MODEL_NAME=Qwen3-8B
MODEL_PATH=models/Qwen3-8B-Q4_K_M.gguf
MODEL_FORMAT=gguf
QUANTIZATION=Q4_K_M
# Matches the Mac-default .env.example's own MAX_CONTEXT -- see
# llm_decompose.py's own comment on that constant for why 8192 stopped being
# enough once _SYSTEM_PROMPT grew to ~6850 tokens (confirmed via the real
# tokenizer). An 8GB RTX 4060 has comfortable VRAM headroom for 16384's larger
# KV cache on top of a Q4_K_M 8B model -- this isn't the tight budget the
# original GGML_ASSERT context-sizing crash on this hardware was about.
MAX_CONTEXT=16384
TEMPERATURE=0.1

# -1 = offload every layer to the GPU. Requires the CUDA-enabled
# llama-cpp-python build from SETUP_RTX4060.md -- a plain `pip install` build
# has no CUDA kernels and will ignore this (falls back to CPU, just slower,
# never crashes).
N_GPU_LAYERS=-1

# Modern gaming-laptop CPUs vary (6-16+ cores). Don't guess a number here --
# on the actual laptop, check your real core count first:
#   Windows (PowerShell): (Get-CimInstance Win32_Processor).NumberOfCores
#   WSL2/Linux:            nproc
# then set N_THREADS to that count (or leave it unset to use llama.cpp's own
# default -- with full GPU offload, CPU threads matter far less anyway since
# the model barely touches the CPU).
# N_THREADS=8
